{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Definition.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Definition.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"recursive definition recursive definition","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Definition.md","fileName":"Recursive Definition.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Recursive Definition","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions","Mathematical Induction","Strong Induction","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Definition.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Definition.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"recursive definition recursive definition","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Definition.md","fileName":"Recursive Definition.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Recursive Definition","heading":"Recursive Definition","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A recursive definition specifies initial values and then defines later or larger objects in terms of earlier or smaller ones. The factorial definition fixes 0! = 1 and defines n! = n(n − 1)! for n > 0; the Fibonacci sequence requires two initial values before applying its recurrence.\n\nBecause the definition builds an infinite family from finite rules, facts about recursively defined objects are commonly proved with [[Mathematical Induction]] or [[Strong Induction]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions","Mathematical Induction","Strong Induction","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Definition.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Re